.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
is a read-only macOS diagnostics CLI for process, binary, signing,
policy, panic, persistence, VPN, and unified-log triage.
It is designed for defensive introspection and evidence-driven output.
.Pp
.Nm
does not bypass SIP, AMFI, TCC, Gatekeeper, taskgated, EndpointSecurity,
or hardened runtime policy.
It reports observed evidence and next checks rather than modifying system
security state.
.Sh COMMANDS
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Cm version Op Fl -json
Show build version, commit, build date, Go version, and target platform.
.It Cm specs Op Fl -json
List Mac model, processor, physical and logical CPU cores, memory, architecture,
graphics processors, and macOS version/build using system_profiler, sysctl,
and sw_vers.
Serial numbers, UUIDs, display identifiers, and hostnames are omitted.
GPU fields depend on available hardware data.
Native and execution architectures are distinguished when they differ.
Requires macOS; no sudo or additional permissions are needed.
.It Cm disk Op Fl -json Op Fl -all Op Fl -full Op Ar path
List size, used space, free space, and capacity percentage for mounted local
disks using df.
A supplied path selects its containing volume.
.Fl -all
includes auxiliary system and virtual local mounts.
.Fl -full
preserves usernames in human-readable paths; JSON always includes raw paths.
Human sizes use binary units, while JSON uses byte counts at 1024-byte
precision.
APFS volumes can share capacity and free space; do not sum rows.
The capacity percentage comes from df and is not used space divided by
total size.
Requires macOS; no sudo is needed.
A supplied path must be accessible.
.It Cm macho Op Fl -json Op Fl -full Op Fl -triage Ar path
Inspect a Mach-O binary or app bundle.
The command reports file identity, architectures, signing, Gatekeeper
assessment, extended attributes, linked libraries, and evidence-backed
findings.
.Fl -triage
flag prints a compact 0-100 score with signals and next actions.
.It Cm proc Op Fl -json Op Fl -maps Op Fl -tree Op Fl -full Ar pid-or-name
Resolve a running process using native process tools, summarize identity
fields, and check executable signing when a path is available.
.Fl -maps
adds virtual region ranges, sizes, protections, sharing modes, and image
mappings from vmmap -w. These are not memory payloads or a complete dyld inventory.
Sizes are virtual spans, not resident RAM. Task access may be denied.
.Fl -tree
adds ancestors and direct children from ps, including paths and start times.
Start times use local time with second resolution; snapshots are not atomic.
.Fl -full
preserves home paths and includes raw vmmap output. JSON retains parsed paths.
.It Cm ipc Op Fl -json Op Fl -full Ar pid-or-name
Show process Mach port rights, queue limits, message counts, and available
peer PIDs/names using lsmp, plus Unix sockets, pipes, FIFOs, POSIX shared-memory
and semaphore descriptors using lsof.
Port names are local to the task, not TCP/UDP port numbers.
Unknown queue values are shown as a dash; zero means a reported zero.
Each source reports ok, partial, or unavailable with explanations.
Available local IPC survives Mach task-access failures.
Requires macOS and task-read access for Mach inspection.
Root can improve visibility, but protected processes may remain inaccessible.
The command does not invoke sudo or modify security policy.
.Fl -full
preserves usernames in paths and includes raw command output.
JSON includes unredacted parsed fields; raw output requires
.Fl -full .
This is a snapshot, not a message trace or an XPC service-name map.
System V IPC and anonymous shared-memory mappings are outside this view.
If both sources are unavailable, the report is emitted with a nonzero exit status.
.It Cm ipc Fl -watch Op Fl -interval Ar duration Op Fl -count Ar N Op Fl -jsonl Ar pid-or-name
Poll one PID/start-time/executable identity, stopping if an observed identity
changes or becomes unavailable. Second-resolution start times cannot rule out
same-second PID reuse. Show added/removed ports, changed rights and metadata,
queue changes, and descriptor changes. Both source snapshots must be ok before
computing deltas; access loss is never interpreted as removal.
The delay between completed snapshots defaults to 2s and must be 250ms to 24h.
.Fl -count
limits snapshots. Ctrl-C stops a watch.
.Fl -jsonl
emits one complete event per line; single-snapshot
.Fl -json
cannot be used with a watch. Polling can miss short-lived events and does not
capture message payloads. The TUI's ipc watch entry streams results until Esc.
.It Cm collect Fl -pid Ar pid Fl -out Ar new-directory Op Fl -reports Ar proc,ipc,maps,tree Op Fl -json
Save selected process reports, raw native command output, and a manifest locally.
All four reports are selected by default. The new destination's parent must
exist; existing paths and symlinks are refused. Directories use mode 0700 and
files mode 0600. The manifest records capture times, versions, source availability,
byte sizes, and SHA-256 hashes of evidence files, excluding the manifest itself.
Hashes detect changes and do not authenticate evidence.
Raw files may contain sensitive paths, arguments, entitlements, and identifiers.
No evidence is uploaded, no sudo is invoked, and no memory payload is saved.
Permission errors and canceled snapshots produce partial evidence.
Write failures preserve completed files and return an error; a successful
manifest path is reported only after it is written.
.Fl -json
emits the manifest while evidence remains on disk. The TUI collect entry prompts
for a PID and a new output directory before writing; rerun prompts again.
.It Cm attach Op Fl -json Op Fl -last Ar duration Ar pid
Explain likely LLDB attach failures using process identity, developer group
membership, signing state, and recent attach-relevant unified logs.
.It Cm persist Op Fl -json Op Fl -dir Ar launchd-dir
Inspect launchd LaunchAgent and LaunchDaemon plists and score suspicious
persistence traits with explicit evidence.
.It Cm tcc Op Fl -json Op Fl -last Ar duration
Parse recent unified logs for TCC and privacy denial evidence.
.It Cm tcc Fl -watch
Stream matching TCC/privacy logs.
.It Cm es Op Fl -json Op Fl -last Ar duration
Parse recent EndpointSecurity entitlement and access-denial logs.
.It Cm es Fl -watch
Stream matching EndpointSecurity logs.
.It Cm vpn Op Fl -json Op Fl -last Ar duration Op Ar vpn-name
Collect VPN service state, utun interfaces, DNS, proxy, route, recent log,
and sleep/wake evidence.
.It Cm panic Fl -last Op Fl -json
Parse the newest panic report under
.Pa /Library/Logs/DiagnosticReports .
.It Cm panic Fl -file Ar panic-file Op Fl -json
Parse a specific panic report.
.It Cm panic Fl -since Ar duration Op Fl -json
Parse panic reports modified within a Go-style duration such as
.Ql 30m
or
.Ql 48h .
.It Cm timeline Fl -pid Ar pid Op Fl -last Ar duration Op Fl -json | Fl -jsonl
Correlate process identity, signing state, and attach/policy-related log
events into normalized timeline events.
.It Cm sysext Op Fl -json
Inventory installed system extensions using
.Xr systemextensionsctl 8
and classify notable states such as multiple active network extensions,
extensions awaiting user approval, and terminated extensions.
.It Cm tui
Launch an interactive full-screen terminal UI browser.
The left pane lists available macscope commands; the right pane shows the output
of the selected command.
Commands execute on demand with results cached during the session.
Navigate with arrow keys or j/k, press enter to run, tab to switch focus, and esc/q to exit.
Prompted commands request a value interactively.
The TUI requires an interactive terminal. Its collect entry writes raw evidence
only to the explicitly supplied new directory; other entries are read-only.
Read-only selections load automatically after a short navigation delay.
Cached results appear immediately; r refreshes them. PID/path selections open
their prompt automatically. Arrow keys can leave an empty automatic prompt.
Esc stops pending collection or dismisses a prompt. Changing selections cancels
a read-only collector before starting another; explicit evidence exports finish.
The collect entry requires Enter before asking for its PID and destination.
.It Cm completion Ar bash | zsh | fish
Generate shell completion scripts.
.El
.Sh OUTPUT
Human output is concise and evidence-driven.
Commands that produce structured reports support
.Fl -json
where noted.
.Cm timeline
command also supports
.Fl -jsonl
for one normalized event per line.
.Pp
Set
.Ev NO_COLOR=1
or
.Ev MACSCOPE_NO_COLOR=1
to disable ANSI styling in human output.
.Sh ENVIRONMENT
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Ev NO_COLOR
Disable ANSI color when set to any value.
.It Ev MACSCOPE_NO_COLOR
Disable ANSI color when set to any value.
.El
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Pa /Library/Logs/DiagnosticReports/*.panic
Default panic report search path.
.It Pa /Library/LaunchAgents
Default launchd persistence search path.
.It Pa /Library/LaunchDaemons
Default launchd persistence search path.
.It Pa ~/Library/LaunchAgents
Default per-user launchd persistence search path.
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Ex -std
.Pp
.Nm
returns 2 for command-line usage errors and unknown commands.
.Sh EXAMPLES
.Bd -literal -offset indent
macscope specs
macscope disk
macscope disk --json /Volumes/Backup
macscope macho --triage /bin/ls
macscope panic --file testdata/panic/watchdog.panic
macscope proc WindowServer
macscope attach 123
macscope persist --json
macscope tcc --last 30m
macscope vpn
